{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And you shall call his name Yitzhak.</b> This name was given to him by the Holy One, blessed be He, and for this reason his name was never changed to call him by another name, as we find with Avraham, whose name was changed from Avram to Avraham, and likewise with Yaakov, whose name was changed from Yaakov to Yisrael — because the name in each of their cases was given through their father; but Yitzhak, whose name was given by the Holy One, blessed be He, that name was fixed for him forever and was not changed. And so I found in Yerushalmi Berakhot: why was the name of Avraham changed and the name of Yaakov changed, but the name of Yitzhak was not changed? Rather, their fathers called them, but Yitzhak — the Holy One, blessed be He, called him, as it is said, ״וקראת את שמו יצחק״ — end quote. And what you find — his name of Yitzhak [written] with a שי״ן, as it is written (Psalms 105:9): ״ושבועתו לישחק״ — this is not a change, but the prophet wished to allude to his attribute, which is the great fire. And you will know this from what they said in Sefer Yetzirah: three mothers אמ״ש — air, water, fire; מ״ם humming, די״ץ hissing. And another reason: Avram required Scripture to change [his name] to Avraham, on account of ״כי אב המון גוים נתתיך״, and Yaakov to Yisrael on account of ״כי שרית עם אלהים״, and because of the circumstances and events that befell them both, their names needed to be changed; but Yitzhak did not need this, for no events like theirs befell him, nor did he find himself driven outside by circumstances as did Avraham and Yaakov, but from the day he was born he was sanctified with the sign of the holy covenant and settled in the Land of Israel, and never departed from it forever:"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:57.693084428Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}